            <ArticleTitle>Clinical review of Crohn's disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>392-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This clinical review presents proposed theories regarding the etiology of Crohn's disease (CD), the pathophysiology of the disorder, and current diagnostic methods.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pertinent publications in the literature, the Crohn's and Colitis Foundation web page, and relevant texts regarding pathophysiology of the gastrointestinal system.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">CD can be a devastating disease and difficult to diagnose. The advanced practice nurse (APRN) should be aware of the etiology, pathophysiology, diagnostic methods, and current treatment options of this disorder.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="IMPLICATIONS FOR PRACTICE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In collaboration with a gastroenterologist, APRNs can provide much needed information to the patient with CD. Practice recommendations include patient education, pain management, and support for quality of life issues.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
